[FEN "8/8/2k1p2p/1p2P3/1P3KNP/8/8/1b6 w - - 0 1"]

 { Barriers are quite common in bishop vs. knight endings, as in this simple example. } 1. { -KEY- } Nf6 $1 { Creating a barrier in order to prevent the entrance of the black king. } ( { In contrast, after } 1. Nxh6 $6 Kd5 { Black obtains counterplay - Baburin. It is worth noting that in most bishop vs. knight endings, the existence of mutual passed pawns favours the bishop. } ) 
Bc2 2. Ke3 { Next, the white king should temporarily replace the knight in the task of controlling d5. } Bf5 3. Kd4 Kb6 ( { Or } 3... Bc2 4. Ng8 $1 h5 5. Nf6 Bd1 6. Ke3 { with similar play - Baburin. } ) 
4. Ng8 $1 { Provocation - once the h-pawn moves to h5 it will be easier for White to attack it; besides, the g5-square is liberated for her king. } h5 5. Ke3 Kc6 6. Nf6 Bg6 7. Kf4 Bc2 8. Kg5 ( { Again there is no reason to go for } 8. Nxh5 $6 Kd5 { either here or on the following moves. } ) 
Bd1 9. Kg6 $1 { Heading for the e6-pawn. } Bf3 10. Kf7 Bg4 11. Ke7 { Zugzwang. } Bf5 ( { On } 11... Kc7 { White could triangulate by } 12. Ke8 Kc6 13. Kf7 { so as to force } Kc7 { after which } 14. Kg6 Kc6 15. Nxh5 { wins comfortably now that } Kd5 { fails to } 16. Nf6 ) 
12. Nxh5 Kd5 13. Kf6 ( { In comparison to the } 8. Nxh5 { subline, here White's king is much more active, which secures a swift win. } ) 
Kc4 14. Ng7 Bd3 15. Nxe6 Kxb4 16. Nd4 Kc5 17. e6 $1 Kd6 18. e7 Kd7 19. Ne6 Bg6 20. Nf8+ 
 1-0

