 { Here is a similar, but more complex example. White has a more active game, and now intends g2-g4-g5, conquering the d5-pawn. Petrosian found a surprising defence. } 1... { -KEY- } Ng8 $1 { Deliberately handing over the d5-pawn in order to set up a barrier. } ( { Another option was } 1... Ne8 $5 2. Rxd5 ( { while } 1... Ng8 1. Ba4 $5 Rd8 3. Bxe8 { can be met by } f6 $1 { and only then 4...Rxe8. } ) 
Rxd5 3. Bxd5 Nd6 { with similar play. } ) 
2. Rxd5 ( { Or } 2. Bxd5 $6 Ne7 { followed by 3...Rxd4. } ) 
Rxd5 3. Bxd5 Ne7 $1 { Heading for c8-d6. } 4. Be4 Nc8 5. Ke3 g5 ( { The immediate } 5... Nd6 { was fine as well, but Petrosian prefers to grab some space. } ) 
6. hxg5 hxg5 7. f4 gxf4+ 8. Kxf4 f6 9. Bd3 Nd6 { The barrier is complete, preventing White from making progress. } 10. Kg4 Kg7 11. g3 Kf7 12. Kh5 Kg7 13. g4 ( { Play might have continued } 13. g4 Kf7 14. g5 fxg5 15. Kxg5 Ke7 16. Kf4 Kd7 17. Ke5 Kc7 { followed by endless knight moves. } ) 

 1/2-1/2

