 { Thanks to its tactical potential (i.e. forks) , the knight can sometimes set up a barrier on its own, as in the next example.With the knight on h2 this would be an elementary draw, but that can't be accomplished here. Fortunately, a different defensive idea is available. } 1. { -KEY- } Ng3 $1 { Creating a barrier against the black king. } ( { In contrast, going for a fork by } 1. Nh6 $2 h2 2. Ng4 { fails to } h1=Q { queening with check - Dvoretsky. } ) 
h2 ( { The direct } 1... Ke3 { allows } 2. Nf1 Kf2 3. Nh2 { with a simple draw; e.g. } Kg2 4. Ng4 Kg3 5. Ne3 { etc. } ) 
2. Kb7 { It transpires that Black's king can't step on e4 or e2, while e3 and d2 aren't available either due to a fork on f1. } Kd4 ( { After } 2... Kc2 3. Kc6 Kd1 4. Kd5 Ke1 5. Ke4 Kf2 6. Nh1 ( { or } 5... Kd4 5. Kf4 ) 
Kg2 7. Ke3 Kxh1 8. Kf2 { it is again a draw. } ) 
3. Kc6 Ke5 4. Kc5 Kf4 5. Nh1 $1 { The knight is sacrificed to leave Black's king trapped in the corner. } Kf3 6. Kd4 Kg2 7. Ke3 Kxh1 8. Kf2 { Stalemate. } 
 1/2-1/2

