 { Here are two more examples of a knight successfully creating a barrier.How to cope with the enemy plan of ...Ke4-d3, finishing off the c3-pawn? } 1. { -KEY- } Na8 $1 { A surprising move, but it turns out that the knight is needed on b6, and this is the only available route. } ( { a) Both } 1. Kg2 $2 Ke4 2. Kg3 Kd3 3. Nb5 ( { or } 3. Nd5 ) 
Kc4 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1. Nb5 $2 Kd5 $1 ( { not } 1. Na8 Ke4 $2 2. c4 Kd3 3. Nd6 $1 { etc. } ) 
2. Na3 Ke4 3. Kg2 Kd3 4. c4 Kc3 5. Kf3 Kb3 6. Ke4 Kxa3 { lead to a draw. But note that with the white king starting on h2, he would win in this line as well, by 7.Kd6 at this moment. } ) 
Kd5 ( { Or } 1... Ke4 2. c4 Kd4 3. Nb6 { securing the pawn. Incidentally, defending the pawn from the front tends to be a good idea in knight vs. pawn endings with blocked pawns. In contrast, without the c5-pawn on the board, the knight should defend the pawn from the back; e.g. from b2 in this case. } ) 
2. Nb6+ Kc6 3. Nc4 Kd5 4. Nd2 $1 { Completing the barrier, with an easy win. } 
 1-0

