 { A clever knight manoeuvre helped White to capitalize on his extra pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Nb3 $1 { Heading for c6 in order to create a barrier together with the d5-pawn. } ( 1. Nf3 { in similar spirit works as well, as does the immediate 2.Na5 on the next move. } ) 
Ke5 2. Kd3 Bf2 3. Na5 Kf5 4. Nc6 Bb6 5. a4 { Preparing to fix the a-pawns on squares of the 'right' colour in relation to the enemy bishop. } a5 { Black plays this move voluntarily, without waiting for White to provoke it by Nb4 or Nb8. } 6. c5 $1 { A strong breakthrough which clears a path for the king via c4-b5. } ( { Alternatively, } 6. Nb8 { followed by Nd7 and c4-c5, was a more complex winning procedure - Muller. } ) 
dxc5 ( { Or } 6... Bxc5 7. Nxa5 Ke5 8. Kc4 { with the mortal threat of Nc6+ and a4-a5-a6-a7 - Ribli. } ) 
7. Kc4 { Thanks to the barrier, White can use his king for active means. } Kf6 ( { If } 7... Ke4 { then } 8. d6 { - Ribli. } ) 
8. Kb5 ( { Ribli also suggests } 8. d6 $5 Ke6 9. Kb5 ) 
c4 ( { Seeing that } 8... Bc7 9. Kxc5 { followed by 10 d6, is hopeless for him, Black makes a desperate try. } ) 
9. Kxb6 ( { Of course } 9. Kxc4 { was also fine. } ) 
c3 10. Nxa5 { Heading for b3, so Black resigned. } 
 1-0
